<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cyfraith Hywel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Laws of Hywel Dda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cyfraith Hywel, the law of Hywel, was the native law of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, traditionally said to have been codified under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hywel-dda">Hywel Dda</a> in the 10th century. Surviving in law books in Welsh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> from the 13th century, it set rules on inheritance, compensation for killing and injury, status, marriage and land, and stayed partly in use until the Laws in Wales Acts of the 16th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cyfraith-hywel.jpg" alt="Cyfraith Hywel" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Proclaiming_the_Laws_of_Howel_Dda.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
